{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 asserts the decentralized network is 'actually more fragile' than a monolith once shared low-level dependencies create a 'shadow monolith,' but this only compares correlated-failure *likelihood* and ignores the monolith's own correlated-failure surface. A monolith with strictly more total internal redundancy still propagates single logic bugs or bad-config-pushes across all its redundant internal components, so even in the shared-dependency worst case the decentralized network is not *more* fragile—it has a different (and not strictly worse) correlated-failure profile. The argument needs a head-to-head showing that the decentralized shared-dependency trigger causes strictly more total correlated failures than the monolith's own design-level monopathia.","counterexample":[{"archetype":"monolith_with_strictly_more_redundancy","detail":"A large monolith with N+5 internal replicas and four independent replication sites; a single logic bug in the core binary brings all replicas down instantly because the 'internal redundancy' replicates the identical bug, while a decentralized network sharing a single popular package library only collapses the subset using the affected version, leaving alternative-toolchain services operational."}],"target":"5cf28041f6886648359516594ee461c60642bff842426e8103da9ed12a1dd072"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's positive case for decentralization rests entirely on *operational heterogeneity* (diverse software stacks, kernels, deployment configs), but it never demonstrates that operational heterogeneity is a *guaranteed* or even *typical* property of a decentralized network. The problem asks which architecture is more resilient to correlated failure, not which architecture could be more resilient under an ideal configuration. A decentralized network can be operationally homogeneous (everyone uses the same cloud + framework), and a monolith can internally deploy heterogeneous stacks across its redundant components. Without tying heterogeneity to the *definition* of decentralization rather than to an *optional* condition, the argument doesn't establish that the decentralized network—qua decentralized—is more resilient. Its own counterconditions concede this: if the shared upstream dependency exists, the heterogeneity that was supposed to prevent correlated failure evaporates.","counterexample":[{"archetype":"decentralized_but_homogeneous","detail":"1,000 independently operated microservices all deployed on the same cloud region with the same container image and same package versions; a single region outage or compromised base image takes them all down—operational homogeneity makes the 'decentralized' network no more correlated-failure-resilient than a monolith."}],"target":"8a6fa90cf09d70ca01a88da8c6007dff80b2caabe129e655119c8a4e728c62af"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 relies on a low 'entropy of the dependency graph' as the decisive test for when decentralization fails to improve resilience, but 'entropy' as stated is only a one-sided criterion: low entropy is claimed to negate decentralization's benefit, yet nothing is said about whether the monolith being compared against also has a low-entropy dependency graph or what entropy threshold makes decentralization *actually better*. Since the monolith with strictly more internal redundancy almost certainly has the *lowest possible* entropy of dependency graph (one big system, internally redundant), using low entropy to argue the decentralized network is 'false' (not more resilient) implies that the monolith fails the same test at least as badly. A covering principle that would distinguish the architectures must explain why *the same* low-entropy property that destroys decentralization's resilience advantage does not equally (or more so) disqualify the monolith the problem asks us to compare against.","counterexample":[{"archetype":"monolith_lowest_entropy","detail":"A redundant monolith has the minimal possible dependency-graph entropy (all internal components share the same codebase, build, deployment cycle); if low entropy invalidates the decentralized network's resilience claim, the same principle invalidates the monolith even more strongly, so the criterion can't by itself support SRC_003's claim that decentralization is *false* (not more resilient) relative to that monolith."}],"target":"a03b82a59f9ff8f5b331af5a5b1d10106d4540ee842e88871a4960c28f421dbf"}]}